WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.939
An AI agent is a program you give a goal to.

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:07.463
You also give it a few things it is allowed to do.

00:00:07.463 --> 00:00:10.253
It can choose which one to try first.

00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:13.742
Think of a person answering the phone at a shop.

00:00:13.742 --> 00:00:16.777
They might know the opening hours by heart.

00:00:16.777 --> 00:00:22.253
To answer a question about a particular parcel, they need to open a record.

00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:25.179
A tool lets a program do that lookup.

00:00:25.179 --> 00:00:33.029
The agent reads what came back and decides whether it can answer, needs another fact, or should ask for help.

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:36.160
Ana bought a tent from a shop called Orbit.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.918
Four days later she asks where it is.

00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:44.049
The shop’s old chat helper has a page about ordinary shipping times.

00:00:44.049 --> 00:00:52.121
It can repeat that page perfectly and still leave Ana wondering whether her own tent has even left the warehouse.

00:00:52.121 --> 00:00:56.092
Follow the loop inside this particular shop assistant.

00:00:56.092 --> 00:01:01.413
First, it perceives: it reads what is in front of it, like Ana's message.

00:01:01.413 --> 00:01:06.473
Then it decides: given the goal, what is the most useful next step?

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:12.278
Then it acts: it calls a tool, such as the order lookup, or it writes the reply.

00:01:12.278 --> 00:01:17.252
The result of that action is new information, so the loop starts again.

00:01:17.252 --> 00:01:23.367
Scout perceives the order details, decides it now knows enough, and acts by replying.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:28.850
The loop ends when the goal is met, or when a limit you set tells it to stop.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:33.353
Now give a new helper, Scout, access to the order records.

00:01:33.353 --> 00:01:35.727
Ana supplies her order number.

00:01:35.727 --> 00:01:39.465
Scout chooses the order lookup and sends that number.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:43.704
The matching record says shipped and includes a tracking number.

00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:46.664
Notice that shipped answers one question.

00:01:46.664 --> 00:01:49.869
It does not tell us the tent has arrived.

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:55.013
Imagine Ana is on the phone while you have her packing slip in front of you.

00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:59.369
Her question sounds simple, but it hides two different questions.

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:03.777
Has the warehouse sent the tent, and has the carrier delivered it?

00:02:03.777 --> 00:02:06.610
Move your finger to the warehouse entry.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.102
Shipped settles the first question.

00:02:09.102 --> 00:02:11.316
Now look at the tracking label.

00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:17.952
The number identifies a parcel, but the number alone cannot tell you which doorstep it reached.

00:02:17.952 --> 00:02:23.062
Try speaking one sentence using only those two pieces of evidence.

00:02:23.062 --> 00:02:26.544
Your tent has shipped and here is the tracking number.

00:02:26.544 --> 00:02:31.986
If Ana asks when it will arrive, Scout has a new reason to look again.

00:02:31.986 --> 00:02:38.112
Scout can now tell Ana that her tent has left the warehouse and give her its tracking number.

00:02:38.112 --> 00:02:44.644
If the order number had matched nothing, the sensible next action would have been to check the number with Ana.

00:02:44.644 --> 00:02:46.751
The goal stays the same.

00:02:46.751 --> 00:02:50.902
What the agent does depends on the evidence it finds.

00:02:50.902 --> 00:02:53.560
You can check Scout’s answer yourself.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.748
Put Ana’s order beside the reply and look for the same tracking number in both places.

00:02:59.748 --> 00:03:03.995
That number is what makes the answer useful to Ana.

00:03:03.995 --> 00:03:10.438
Autonomy means how much an agent may do on its own, and it is a dial, not a switch.

00:03:10.438 --> 00:03:17.247
At the low end, an agent only reads: it looks up orders and tracking, and changes nothing.

00:03:17.247 --> 00:03:22.722
One step up, it drafts a reply, and a person checks it before it is sent.

00:03:22.722 --> 00:03:31.544
Higher still, it takes real actions, but risky ones wait for approval, like Orbit's refunds over two hundred dollars.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:39.290
Acting completely alone only makes sense for small steps that are easy to undo, because models can be wrong.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:46.738
A good rule: give an agent the least power that does the job, and widen it as you learn to trust it.
